                <pb facs="0759" n="751" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg751"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/2093: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Butrick</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-02T14:00:00+09:00">July 2, 1940—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 2—11:57 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>584. In continuation of my 583, July 2, 1 p.m., the French Consul General stated
                    that he would take orders from the Pétain Government. He said he had not been
                    approached by any German authority but that in case of necessity he would prefer
                    dealing direct with German representative rather than through an intermediary.
                    He said if necessary to give up the French Concession he and his Ambassador were
                    determined to return it to the Chungking Government, which he felt might put the
                    Settlement in somewhat of a predicament. Such course of action would doubtless
                    mean the occupation of the Concession by the Japanese.</p>
                <p>Admiral Hart who is scheduled to arrive today will be informed.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department. Repeated to Chungking, Peiping. Code text by air mail to
                    Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Butrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
